  "summary": "Hyostylic: the palatoquadrate hangs from the braincase on the hyomandibula, so the whole upper-jaw complex can slide forward (protrusion) as well as the lower jaw swinging open.",
  "note": "Outlines, lever arms and ranges of motion are schematic. The muscles, their attachments and the action each one is credited with come from Huber et al. (2011) Table 2 and its text; tests/sim.test.js checks that the geometry reproduces those actions. Upper-jaw protrusion is drawn as a forward slide of the palatoquadrate complex along the underside of the braincase, because that is the movement the preorbitalis is described as producing and a single hinge cannot be made to do it. The hyomandibula is drawn as a rod that follows the jaw joint rather than a separate degree of freedom.",
