      "sources": [
        "wilga-etal-2001",
        "ziermann-etal-2014",
        "wilga-2005",
        "huber-etal-2011"
      ],
      "actionText": "Protrudes the upper jaw: it pulls the back of the palatoquadrate forward, so the front slides down a groove in the braincase. Recorded active, with the quadratomandibularis, as the jaws close.",
      "note": "Wilga et al. (2001) report preorbitalis activity and upper-jaw protrusion during jaw closure in Squalus, not during opening, and give the lower-jaw lift to the quadratomandibularis, which pulls the upper jaw toward the rising lower jaw. They do not describe a stabilising role. Huber et al. (2011) credit the muscle with elevating the lower jaw as well; the model draws no direct jaw-closing action for it, and the lower jaw simply rides with the palatoquadrate. Wilga (2005, abstract only) calls it the primary protractor in squalean sharks. Ziermann et al. (2014), from their own dissections, run it from a tendon on Meckel's cartilage to the ethmoid region of the braincase; Wilga et al. give nasal capsule and quadratomandibularis; Huber et al. the raphe of the adductor. The drawing follows Wilga et al. for the cranial end; the insertion is a raphe inside the adductor, on its own segment that follows the jaw at half its angle. No muscle record yet; the dataset parks the row.",
      "expect": [
        {
          "dof": "upper-jaw",
          "dir": 1
        }
      ]
    },
